-- title:
--   Definition 4.1 — good answers and $(\kappa,e,p_{\mathrm{ans}})$-oracles; dual local norm, Hessian and Newton decrement (46)
-- statement:
--   Fix symmetric data $A_1,\dots,A_m,C\in\mathbb S^n$, a real $q>2$, the slack $S_x=\sum_i x_iA_i-C$, the domain $\mathcal D=\{x\in\mathbb R^m:S_x\succ0\}$, the matrix Lewis barrier $f_q$ and the Lewis metric $M_u$ of Definition 3.14.
--
--   1. For a positive definite $H\in\mathbb S^m$, the **dual local norm** of $v\in\mathbb R^m$ is
--   $$\|v\|_{H,*}:=\sup\{v^\top h:h^\top Hh\le1\}=\sqrt{v^\top H^{-1}v}.$$
--   2. $H_x:=\nabla^2f_q(x)$ is the Hessian of $f_q$ at $x\in\mathcal D$, and, for $c'\in\mathbb R^m$ and $\mu>0$, the **Newton decrement** of the path objective $F_\mu(x)=c'^\top x/\mu+f_q(x)$ is
--   $$\lambda_{c'}(x,\mu):=\Bigl\|\frac{c'}{\mu}+\nabla f_q(x)\Bigr\|_{H_x,*}.$$
--   3. A **query** is a point $u\in\mathbb R^m$ with a flag for a metric request. An **answer** is FAIL or a vector $g\in\mathbb R^m$, with a matrix $N\in\mathbb S^m$ if a metric is requested. It is **$(\kappa,e)$-good** if $u\in\mathcal D$, it is not FAIL, $\|g-\nabla f_q(u)\|_{M_u,*}\le e$, and $\kappa^{-1}M_u\preceq N\preceq\kappa M_u$ if a metric is requested.
--   4. A **$(\kappa,e,p_{\mathrm{ans}})$-oracle** gives each query at a point of $\mathcal D$ a $(\kappa,e)$-good answer with probability at least $1-p_{\mathrm{ans}}$, conditionally on all earlier random draws. The draws live on a probability space $(\Omega,\mu)$ with a filtration $(\mathcal F_t)$, $\mathcal F_t$ generated by the draws before query $t$; the oracle is a map $O$ with $O_t(\omega,Q)$ the answer to the $t$-th query $Q$. For every $\mathcal F_t$-measurable query $Q_t$, the answer $O_t(\cdot,Q_t)$ is $\mathcal F_{t+1}$-measurable, and almost surely on $\{Q_t\in\mathcal D\}$,
--   $$\Pr\bigl[O_t(\cdot,Q_t)\text{ is }(\kappa,e)\text{-good}\bigm|\mathcal F_t\bigr]\ge1-p_{\mathrm{ans}}.$$
--
--   These are the interface between the path-following method of Section 4.1 and the sketched oracle that computes matrix Lewis weights approximately.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The dual norm is written in its closed form $\sqrt{v^\top H^{-1}v}$, and it is only read at $M_u$ and $H_x$ for points of $\mathcal D$, which are positive definite there. $X\preceq Y$ is `(Y - X).PosSemidef`. An answer is one record `(fail, g, N)`; the σ-algebra on answers is the one of these fields. The conditional probability is the conditional expectation of the indicator of the good event. $p_{\mathrm{ans}}\in[0,1)$ is a hypothesis of the theorems.
-- source:
--   Kelner, Jiang, Sidford, Zhang, Matrix Lewis Weights: Solving Semidefinite Programs in Square Root Constraints Iterations, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 31ce765b…), Definition 4.1, p.35; dual local norm and H_x, pp.35–36; Equation (46), p.36

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MatrixLewis_slack
import Definitions.Def_MatrixLewis_barrier
import Definitions.Def_MatrixLewis_localNormSq

open MeasureTheory

namespace MatrixLewis

namespace Supplement

/-- The dual local norm `‖v‖_{H,*} := sup{v⊤h : h⊤Hh ≤ 1} = √(v⊤H⁻¹v)` of a vector `v ∈ ℝᵐ` for a
positive definite `H ∈ Sᵐ` (Kelner–Jiang–Sidford–Zhang, *Matrix Lewis Weights: Solving Semidefinite
Programs in Square Root Constraints Iterations*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 31ce765b…),
§4.1, pp.35–36, used in Definition 4.1 and Equation (46)).

**Formalization Note** Written as `√(v⊤H⁻¹v)`, the closed form the paper uses for positive definite
`H`. Every use evaluates it at the Lewis metric `M_u` or the Hessian `H_x` of a point of `𝒟`, which are
positive definite there (`H_x ⪰ M_x ≻ 0`, Theorem 3.17 and Definition 3.14). -/
noncomputable def dualNorm {m : ℕ} (H : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ) (v : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m)) :
    ℝ :=
  Real.sqrt (dotProduct (WithLp.ofLp v) (H⁻¹.mulVec (WithLp.ofLp v)))

/-- The Hessian `H_x := ∇²f_q(x)` of the matrix Lewis barrier as an `m × m` matrix,
`(H_x)ᵢⱼ = D²f_q(x)[eᵢ, eⱼ]` (Kelner–Jiang–Sidford–Zhang, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256
31ce765b…), §4.1, p.35). Read only at `x ∈ 𝒟`, where `f_q` is real analytic (Theorem 3.9). -/
noncomputable def hessMat {n m : ℕ} (q : ℝ) (A : Fin m → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ)
    (C : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (x : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m)) : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ :=
  Matrix.of fun i j =>
    iteratedFDeriv ℝ 2 (barrier q A C) x ![EuclideanSpace.single i 1, EuclideanSpace.single j 1]

/-- The Newton decrement of the path objective `F_μ(x) := c′⊤x/μ + f_q(x)` (Kelner–Jiang–Sidford–Zhang,
STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 31ce765b…), Equation (46), p.36):
`λ_{c′}(x, μ) := ‖c′/μ + ∇f_q(x)‖_{H_x,*}`, with `H_x = ∇²f_q(x)`.

**Formalization Note** `∇f_q(x)` is Mathlib's `gradient` on `ℝᵐ = EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m)` (the
vector of partial derivatives). Read only at `x ∈ 𝒟`. -/
noncomputable def newtonDec {n m : ℕ} (q : ℝ) (A : Fin m → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ)
    (C : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (c' : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m)) (x : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m))
    (μ : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  dualNorm (hessMat q A C x) (μ⁻¹ • c' + gradient (barrier q A C) x)

/-- A query (Definition 4.1, p.35): a point `u ∈ ℝᵐ` with a flag for a metric request
(`true`: a metric is requested). -/
abbrev Query (m : ℕ) : Type := EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m) × Bool

/-- An answer (Definition 4.1, p.35): `FAIL` (`fail = true`) or a vector `g ∈ ℝᵐ`, with a matrix
`N ∈ Sᵐ` when a metric is requested.

**Formalization Note** One record covers every case: when `fail = true` the other fields are
ignored, and on a query without a metric request `metric` is ignored. -/
structure Answer (m : ℕ) where
  /-- `true` for the answer `FAIL` -/
  fail : Bool
  /-- the gradient answer `g` -/
  grad : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m)
  /-- the metric answer `N` (read only after a metric request) -/
  metric : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ

/-- The σ-algebra on answers: the one induced by the fields `(fail, g, N)`, with `N` read as the
array of its entries (Borel on each). Needed only to say that an answer depends measurably on the
random draws (Definition 4.1). -/
noncomputable instance instMeasurableSpaceAnswer (m : ℕ) : MeasurableSpace (Answer m) :=
  MeasurableSpace.comap
    (fun a : Answer m => ((a.fail, a.grad, fun i j => a.metric i j) :
      Bool × EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin m) × (Fin m → Fin m → ℝ)))
    inferInstance

/-- **Definition 4.1** (Good answers; Kelner–Jiang–Sidford–Zhang, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript
sha256 31ce765b…), p.35). An answer `a` to the query `Q = (u, flag)` is `(κ, e)`-good if `u ∈ 𝒟`, it is
not `FAIL`, `‖g − ∇f_q(u)‖_{M_u,*} ≤ e`, and `κ⁻¹M_u ⪯ N ⪯ κM_u` if a metric is requested; `M_u` is the
Lewis metric at `u` (Definition 3.14, the published `lewisMetric`).

**Formalization Note** `X ⪯ Y` is `(Y − X).PosSemidef` (this includes symmetry, so a good metric
answer is symmetric). -/
def IsGood {n m : ℕ} (q : ℝ) (A : Fin m → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (C : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ)
    (κ e : ℝ) (Q : Query m) (a : Answer m) : Prop :=
  Q.1 ∈ dom A C ∧ a.fail = false ∧
    dualNorm (lewisMetric q A C Q.1) (a.grad - gradient (barrier q A C) Q.1) ≤ e ∧
    (Q.2 = true →
      (a.metric - κ⁻¹ • lewisMetric q A C Q.1).PosSemidef ∧
        (κ • lewisMetric q A C Q.1 - a.metric).PosSemidef)

/-- **Definition 4.1** (Oracles; Kelner–Jiang–Sidford–Zhang, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256
31ce765b…), p.35). A `(κ, e, p_ans)`-oracle gives each query at a point of `𝒟` a `(κ, e)`-good answer
with probability at least `1 − p_ans`, conditionally on all earlier random draws.

The random draws live on a probability space `(Ω, μ)` with a filtration `ℱ`, where `ℱ t` is generated
by the draws before query `t` (`t = 0, 1, …`). The oracle is `O : ℕ → Ω → Query m → Answer m`:
`O t ω Q` is the answer to the `t`-th query when it is `Q` and the draws are `ω`, so an answer may
depend on the query and on every earlier draw, including draws that no earlier answer shows. The two
conditions, for every query `Q : Ω → Query m` that is `ℱ t`-measurable (a function of the draws before
query `t`), are:
1. the answer `ω ↦ O t ω (Q ω)` is `ℱ (t + 1)`-measurable (it is drawn before query `t + 1`);
2. almost surely on the event `Q ∈ 𝒟`, `Pr[answer t is (κ, e)-good | ℱ t] ≥ 1 − p_ans`.

**Formalization Note** The conditional probability is the conditional expectation `μ[1_G | ℱ t]` of
the indicator of the good event `G`. The paper's `p_ans ∈ [0, 1)` is a hypothesis of the statements
that use oracles. -/
def IsOracle {n m : ℕ} (q : ℝ) (A : Fin m → Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ)
    (C : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (κ e pans : ℝ) {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (μ : Measure Ω) (ℱ : Filtration ℕ mΩ) (O : ℕ → Ω → Query m → Answer m) : Prop :=
  ∀ (t : ℕ) (Q : Ω → Query m), Measurable[ℱ t] Q →
    Measurable[ℱ (t + 1)] (fun ω => O t ω (Q ω)) ∧
      ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, (Q ω).1 ∈ dom A C →
        1 - pans ≤
          (μ[({ω' | IsGood q A C κ e (Q ω') (O t ω' (Q ω'))}).indicator (fun _ => (1 : ℝ)) |
            ℱ t]) ω

end Supplement

end MatrixLewis


